Date: Sat, 22 May 93 23:16:31 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Science on the List, Terminology, Traversable Wormholes

This message contains material on three threads: 

- still more on the Chaitin thread, esp. the need to standardize on terms
and on the "basics"

- some further comments on the "Traversable Wormholes" thread. 

- and some comments about the "science editors" of "Extropy" 

Sorry to juxtapose these topics, but at least some of the players are the
same. And there's a deeper link, having to do with how science is done on
our list.


TERMINOLOGY AND BASIC CONCEPTS

A few days ago I expressed my frustration that we in this thread on math,
randomness, Chaitin, Turing machines, algorithmic complexity, etc. are not
always using the commonly agreed-upon terms, the standard meanings of the
language, and that some exposure to the *standard books* would be helpful.
This problem showed up most dramatically in the Mike Price denunciation of
Chaitin's "Scientific American" article, which denunciation Derek
challenged (and has convincingly won), largely because of a failure to use
"computable" in its accurate sense, the sense used in the CS community
these past several decades.

My good friends in this thread (you know who you are--it's _all_ of you!)
are spinning their wheels, and the List's wheels, by not finding out what
the terms are, what the historical arguments have been, and so on. Look,
I'm not trying to sound superior or anything like that...it's just that
*every field* has its core concepts and terms. We see this all the time in
the economics-related threads or in the linguistics-related threads, when
someone will use a commonsense definition (read: _their_ commonsense
definition!) for something that actually has a fairly well-defined meaning.
(This is not to say the commonly-accepted definitions are right, or don't
deserve to be challenged. But people should know precisely what it is
they're challenging.)

This is why we suggest to people that they read the "basic books," such as
"Machinery of Freedom," "The Enterprise of Law," Hayek, Selgin, Rand,
Vinge, whatever, before claiming that "capitalism can't work" or, in the
thread at hand, that "all irrational numbers are uncomputable." "Debating
the basics" is not fruitful. (Debating foundational issues can of course be
very rewarding, and frustrating, but it must be the case that the debaters
have a solid grounding in the field before they debate the foundations. Not
always, but usually. BTW, I say "not always" because I think it perfectly
legitimate for me to challenge a Marxist economics professor on
foundational issues, for example, even if he has 30 years of schooling
behind him. I won't digress further.)

Now I claim that the basic ideas of Turing machines, Godel's Theorem,
computability, intractability, randomness, information theory, etc., have
roughly the same status, the same importance, as such topics as libertarian
politics, the gold standard, vitamins, and the like. People who want to
debate these results or challenge conclusions should speak the standard
language if they wish to be understood and taken seriously. (BTW, if I am
*wrong* on this assumption about the place for these topics, if the List
feels that these subjects and related subjects--such as self-reproducing
machines, the complexity of the mind, genetic programming, etc.--are a
"backwater" of Extropianism, then I will reconsider my displays of
enthusiasm for these topics. Not because they'll interest me less, but
because I'll have a better handle on the interests of the readership out
there. From some e-mail I've gotten, many "lurkers" on this particular
thread have nonetheless had their interest sparked and may dig up some of
the books some of us have cited.)


TRAVERSABLE WORMHOLES

In my posting expressing frustration, I also admitted to having a negative
reaction to the wormholes/exotic matter/stargates/time empires ideas, taken
as a whole (any _one_ of them is worthy of a fanciful debate, but mixing
them all together....). 

But I also admitted I had only skimmed the book "Cosmic Wormholes" and so
was not up to speed enough to make substantive comments, which is
*precisely* why I'd kept my mouth shut on the whole Hanson-Price thread. I
maintain that, from what I have seen, the Matt Visser stuff is highly
speculative--nothing wrong with that, I suppose, if handled right in
"Extropy." But the empires of time, the aliens colonizing the universe by
staking out wormholes which prevent other wormholes ("empire time," if I
have it right), and such extremely speculative things as time travel,
causality violations, negative mass, antigravity, etc., demand *very
careful* treatment.

Mike Price said "Physics tells us what possible and what's not.  But it
doesn't tell us _how_ to do things.  That's engineering." [SCI:
Anti-gravity, 5-20-93] True enough, but I certainly hope Mike is not
implying that on this whole wormhole/empire time thing, the physics is
already solid and now it's just engineering!

My point about the danger of "crankishness" was perhaps a bit too harsh,
and maybe that's what angered Mike so much. To my view, any articles on
pure speculation about time travel and computing or on time travel and
wormholes should be treated with extreme caution! The danger for any
future-oriented publication like "Extropy," especially one seeking news
stand sales, is overhyping and playing fast and loose with the science in
order to grab the attention of finicky readers. This is what I am worrying
about.

I'm concentrating on physics because that's my background--despite my
interest for the last decade or so in other things--and that's where my
"bogosity alarms" ring loudest. 


WHERE ARE THE SCIENCE EDITORS?

And I'd feel a lot better about things if "Extropy"'s "science editors"
were reading and contributing to the discussion on *this list*. The better
to gauge our inputs on what's important, what's fluff, etc., and to
contribute their own ideas to the list. Max More occasionally comments, but
is perhaps too busy doing other things. Ditto for Dave Krieger (Science),
Russell Whitaker (Communications), Simon! D. Levy (Computing, Linguistics),
Greg Benford (Physics...is Benford *really* involved, or just on the
masthead?), Michael Perry (Cryonics), and so on. I am getting the distinct
impression that our debates here on this list bear little or no resemblance
to the stuff printed in "Extropy." This is not meant to be a rant against
the magazine--it can be a lot of fun.

If "Extropy" ever comes to be seen as a crank journal, a pseudoscience rag,
then we can forget about positively influencing the future through it. And
the type of people we may get as subscribers to this list.....

Let me reiterate that I am not saying Mike Price's article is provably
false. Rather, I am just saying that any article centered around exotic
matter and negative mass-energy, wormholes, FTL, "aliens," and time travel
triggers my "Show me!" reflexes in a major way. Merely handwaving about
nanotech brains that think really deep thoughts, really quickly will not
solve the problems connected with wormholes.

I guess I'd feel a lot more comfortable about Mike's article if it was
*very clearly* labeled "wild speculation" or somesuch. Magazines often have
"blue sky" or "unrealizable dreams" sections, especially to "contain" such
articles. This, to me, would defuse some of the dangers.

And why not a "Letters" section for critiques and rebuttals?  

Cheers,

-Tim May
