The big problem in AI is not artificial intelligence, it is artificial 
perception.  We can program computers to solve intelligence tests.  We
cannot program them to make sense of images or sounds.

It is fairly obvious that perception is affected by brain size, but not
strongly affected.  Large long lived hunting spiders perceive the world
much as we do - at least the daylight hunting spiders do.  Night hunters
probably perceive differently, but probably only because their senses are
different, not because their capacity is less.

Thus if the failure of AI is evidence for "souls" it follows that rather
simple animals possess souls.

In my opinion a more likely interpretation is that the neurones employ
some means for processing information that is radically different from
any that we can easily imagine.

