Date: Sun, 23 May 1993 02:51:03 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?

David Friedman:
> So if a successful experiment raises the
> probability that the proposition is true (in this case to 1) a failure must
> lower it.
> ...probabilistic argument (the prediction failed, therefore one should lower
> the probability one assigns to each assumption).

Again I claim this is a fallacy.  My previous argument was based on 
first-order logic, because your original argument stressed "falsification", 
but it is generalizable to fuzzy set theory.   The outcome "AI will 
work in these ways with current computing resources" can be modelled by 
three largely independent probability distributions:

(1) [the mind is mechanistic], with probability p1
(2) [if the mind is mechanistic then AI will work], with probability p2
(3) [if AI will work then it will work using current methods and
computing resources], with probability p3

Showing that their intersection is improbable, ie the fact that AI doesn't 
work with current methods and computing resources, doesn't tell us which of
(1), (2), or (3) is improbable.   On the other hand, if AI was shown
to work with degree of confidence C, then p1, p2, and p3 would all be
greater than C.

Perhaps you had in mind a different probabilistic model of
falsification (improbabilization?) for AI, eg one involving
set union, but I can't think of what it might be.  Can you be more 
explicit?

