PersonallyI think that any dismissal of AI and/or proof of an
existance of a soul should rest only on logical/mathematical grounds
and not on current experiments to replicate the brain.

After all, not only is the brain much more powerful, apparently, that
oops, (change that to than) than current computers, but its architecture
is so different from the computers of today that a _lot_ of overhead
would be used in emulating the architecture of the brain instead of
in doing the actual calculations.

For instance, I'm hooked into usl's computer system with the terminal
program kermit running on an old IBM PC with an 8088 microprocessor.
Assuming that this 8088 doesn't have its current limitations wrt address
space (in fact let's assume it's infinite, since that's the most absurd
thing I can think of for an intel processor of that era) and can hold the
program and data (although the two would probably be interchangable) for
the "New Brain" simulation program, and that I can "bit-blast" the contents
of my brain at a particular moment into the appropriate arrays in the
program/data space.

THEN: I sit and mull something over about this thread for a couple seconds.
Let's just say I'm trying to remember something about the equivalency of
all turing machines (the target of this whole analogy, to be frank).

How long would it take for the 8088-i (i for infinite address space) p
processor to "think" that same thought? Thousands of years? Millions?
Billions? Remember folks, that Turing's thesis basically says that
it will take finite and not infinite time for one turing machine to
model another. Finite time may still be much greater than the current
age of the universe. (Especially since the 8088-i is probably using
more cycles to do the emulation than it is to do the actual "thinking.")

The most parallel computers that humans have built to this date are
probably so much less parallel (ack, let me check a dictionary)
(never mind, I spelled parallel right) than the human brain that
they might as well be classical Von Neumann architecture machines.

I just realized that my comments above about the Turing thesis are
probably of general relevance, so I'm forwarding them to the list.
Does anyone have any comments?

(Note: I'm not saying the Turing Thesis is wrong; simply that it's
_useless_ for many purposes of AI, especially since it may take 
increadibly long times for one architecture to emulate another.
Since we run our model "thinking" programs in computers (either
Von Neumann or (compared to the brain) slightly parallel) this 
represents a major barrier to our attempts to validate or invalidate
many of the assumptions in the field of artifical intelligence
as well as the possibly associated questions of "soulfulness.")

Side comment: I have very strong suspicions of what many here would
call "non-rational" or "non-extropian-correct" that there is a such
thing as a soul. Am I being rational or non-rational in arguing with
David Friedman's thesis that "AI has failed" (to oversimplify his
position) and that therefore we have no evidence that there is a soul?

