After seeing all the other replies and your reply to them, I want to
reiterate that the weakest single point in your argument is your
assumption that there are no assumptions behind the AI prediction
"that we now know with certainty to be false and necessary to the
prediction".
 
It is difficult to know with certainty which assumptions lead to a
given prediction. But there are certainly assumptions evident in early
(and even, unfortunately, current) writing on AI which are clearly
false and can certainly lend support (if true) to predictions of early
and easy achievement of full, strong AI.
 
Some exhibits of such assumptions and attitudes (sorry I don't have
complete citations at the moment):
 
"We'll soon have software which will translate fully and completely
between two natural languages. Such software will not necessarily need
to implement full, strong AI." (Gerard K. O'Neill, 2081)
 
