> I think you are correct, if the limit of your claim is that the fact
> that we have not yet been able to build an intelligent being gives
> some evidence to the claim that we will never be able to. 

The problem is consciousness, rather than intelligence, since the hard
part is perception, which rather simple animals often do quite well.

We now have a better understanding of the problems involved in perception,
and there seems to be no hope of making progress with existing methods.

This does not mean that some radical breakthrough will not arrive tomorrow.

It does mean that artificial consciousness is still a basic science
problem, not an engineering problem.

With current techniques, the computing power required to perceive objects
from pixels, sounds from waveforms, etc, is non polynomially large.  It is
obvious that brains are very far from being non polynomially large, thus
brains are doing something fundamentally different.


